I now have everything needed. Here is my complete report.

## Overview: How `POST /api/owners` works

The flow is defined entirely in YAML (OfficeFloor "composition" flow) plus generated DTOs (from `openapi.yml`) and MapStruct mappers. There is **no traditional controller** — each step is a small Java function class chained via `next:`.

---

## 1. YAML for creating owners (POST owners)

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`**
```yaml
composition:
  authorize: "hasRole('OWNER_ADMIN')"

build:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.BuildOwner
  govern: [ transaction ]
  next: save

save:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.SaveOwner
  govern: [ transaction ]
  next: respond

respond:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.RespondWithOwnerCreated
  govern: [ transaction ]
```

Flow: `BuildOwner` → `SaveOwner` → `RespondWithOwnerCreated`, guarded by `hasRole('OWNER_ADMIN')` and a `transaction` governance.

---

## 2. Java function classes referenced by the YAML

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/BuildOwner.java`**
```java
package org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner;

import jakarta.validation.Valid;
import net.officefloor.plugin.variable.Out;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.mapper.OwnerMapper;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.model.Owner;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto.OwnerFieldsDto;
import org.springframework.validation.annotation.Validated;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestBody;

@Validated
public class BuildOwner {

    public void service(@Valid @RequestBody OwnerFieldsDto request, OwnerMapper ownerMapper, Out<Owner> built) {
        built.set(ownerMapper.toOwner(request));
    }
}
```
Note: `@Valid @RequestBody OwnerFieldsDto` is where bean-validation runs; a validation failure here is what raises `MethodArgumentNotValidException`.

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/SaveOwner.java`**
```java
package org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner;

import net.officefloor.plugin.variable.Val;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.model.Owner;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.repository.OwnerRepository;

public class SaveOwner {

    public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) {
        ownerRepository.save(owner);
    }
}
```

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/RespondWithOwnerCreated.java`**
```java
package org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner;

import java.net.URI;

import net.officefloor.plugin.variable.Val;
import net.officefloor.web.ObjectResponse;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.mapper.OwnerMapper;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.model.Owner;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto.OwnerDto;

public class RespondWithOwnerCreated {

    public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerMapper ownerMapper,
            ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<OwnerDto>> response) {
        OwnerDto dto = ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner);
        response.send(ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/api/owners/" + owner.getId())).body(dto));
    }
}
```

**Mapper: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`**
```java
package org.springframework.samples.petclinic.mapper;

import org.jspecify.annotations.NonNull;
import org.mapstruct.Mapper;
import org.mapstruct.Mapping;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Page;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.model.Owner;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto.OwnerDto;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto.OwnerFieldsDto;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto.OwnerPageDto;

import java.util.Collection;
import java.util.List;

@Mapper(uses = PetMapper.class)
public interface OwnerMapper {

    OwnerDto toOwnerDto(Owner owner);
    Owner toOwner(OwnerDto ownerDto);

    @Mapping(target = "id", ignore = true)
    @Mapping(target = "pets", ignore = true)
    Owner toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto ownerDto);

    List<OwnerDto> toOwnerDtoCollection(Collection<Owner> ownerCollection);
    Collection<Owner> toOwners(Collection<OwnerDto> ownerDtos);

    default OwnerPageDto toOwnerPageDto(@NonNull Page<Owner> ownerPage) {
        OwnerPageDto ownerPageDto = new OwnerPageDto();
        ownerPageDto.setContent(toOwnerDtoCollection(ownerPage.getContent()));
        ownerPageDto.setPage(ownerPage.getNumber());
        ownerPageDto.setSize(ownerPage.getSize());
        ownerPageDto.setTotalElements(ownerPage.getTotalElements());
        ownerPageDto.setTotalPages(ownerPage.getTotalPages());
        return ownerPageDto;
    }
}
```

---

## 3. Owner entity + DTOs (validation annotations)

### Entity: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`
Key validation annotations (extends `Person`):
```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "owners")
public class Owner extends Person {
    @Column(name = "address")
    @NotEmpty
    private String address;

    @Column(name = "city")
    @NotEmpty
    private String city;

    @Column(name = "telephone")
    @NotEmpty
    @Digits(fraction = 0, integer = 10)
    @Pattern(regexp = "^[0-9]{10}$", message = "Phone number must be exactly 10 digits")
    private String telephone;

    @OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL, mappedBy = "owner", fetch = FetchType.EAGER)
    private Set<Pet> pets;
    // ... getters/setters, pet helpers, toString ...
}
```
(The entity's bean-validation is not the one that drives the POST 400 — that is driven by the DTO below during `@Valid` binding. `Person.java` at `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Person.java` holds `firstName`/`lastName`.)

### DTOs are GENERATED from OpenAPI, not hand-written source
There are **no** `OwnerRequest`/`OwnerResponse` classes. The request DTO is `OwnerFieldsDto` and the response DTO is `OwnerDto`, both generated (by the OpenAPI generator) into `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto` from **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml`**. The relevant schemas:

**`OwnerFields` (→ `OwnerFieldsDto`, the POST request body), openapi.yml lines 1991-2039:**
```yaml
    OwnerFields:
      title: Owner fields
      description: Editable fields of a pet owner.
      type: object
      properties:
        firstName:
          type: string
          minLength: 1
          maxLength: 30
          pattern: "^[\\p{L}]+([ '-][\\p{L}]+){0,2}$"
          example: George
        lastName:
          type: string
          minLength: 1
          maxLength: 30
          pattern: "^[\\p{L}]+([ '-][\\p{L}]+){0,2}\\.?$"
          example: Franklin
        address:
          type: string
          minLength: 1
          maxLength: 255
        city:
          type: string
          minLength: 1
          maxLength: 80
        telephone:
          type: string
          minLength: 1
          maxLength: 20
          pattern: '^[0-9]*$'
      required:
        - firstName
        - lastName
        - address
        - city
        - telephone
```
These schema constraints become Jakarta validation annotations (`@NotNull`/`@Size`/`@Pattern`) on the generated `OwnerFieldsDto`, which is what `@Valid` in `BuildOwner` enforces.

**`Owner` (→ `OwnerDto`, the response), openapi.yml lines 2040-2063:** `allOf` of `OwnerFields` plus read-only `id` (int32, min 0) and required read-only `pets` array of `Pet`.

**`ValidationMessage` (→ `ValidationMessageDto`), openapi.yml lines 1954-1967:**
```yaml
    ValidationMessage:
      title: Validation message
      description: Messages describing a validation error.
      type: object
      properties:
        message:
          type: string
          example: "[Path '/lastName'] Instance type (null) does not match any allowed primitive type (allowed: ['string'])"
          readOnly: true
      required:
        - message
      additionalProperties: true
```

---

## 4. Validation escalation handler — how a 400 is produced today

The escalation is wired by YAML file name = exception class name, under `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/escalation/`:

**`org.springframework.web.bind.MethodArgumentNotValidException.yml`**
```yaml
handle:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.ValidationExceptionHandler
```
(Sibling handlers: `java.lang.Exception.yml` → `GeneralExceptionHandler`; `org.springframework.dao.DataIntegrityViolationException.yml` → `DataIntegrityViolationExceptionHandler`; `...NotFoundException.yml` → `NotFoundExceptionHandler`; `...AuthorizationDeniedException.yml` → `ForbiddenExceptionHandler`.)

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/ValidationExceptionHandler.java`**
```java
package org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Objects;

import net.officefloor.plugin.section.clazz.Parameter;
import net.officefloor.web.ObjectResponse;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ProblemDetail;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto.ValidationMessageDto;
import org.springframework.validation.BindingResult;
import org.springframework.web.bind.MethodArgumentNotValidException;

public class ValidationExceptionHandler {

    public void handle(@Parameter MethodArgumentNotValidException ex,
